\subsection{Composition works exactly for conjunctions up to rounding}
The source retains the qualitative phrase about lower-order terms; we do not assign it a new universal asymptotic meaning. Work with fan-in-two AND/OR and unary NOT gates, counting every gate on a longest input-output path. A single input variable has depth zero. Let $\operatorname{AND}_a$ be conjunction of $a\ge1$ inputs.
\begin{proposition}
For arbitrary Boolean functions, substitution of circuits gives $D(f\circ g)\le D(f)+D(g)$. For conjunctions,
\[
D(\operatorname{AND}_a\circ\operatorname{AND}_b)=\lceil\log_2(ab)\rceil,
\]
which differs from $D(\operatorname{AND}_a)+D(\operatorname{AND}_b)$ by at most one.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Replace each input occurrence of a circuit for $f$ by a copy of a circuit for $g$ on its corresponding disjoint block. Every resulting path passes through at most $D(g)$ gates followed by at most $D(f)$, proving the upper bound.
Any output at depth $h$ can depend on at most $2^h$ distinct input variables. This follows by induction: a binary gate takes the union of at most two sets each of size $2^{h-1}$; a unary NOT gate cannot increase the number. Conjunction of $r$ inputs depends on all $r$, because changing one input while the others are 1 changes the output. Its depth is thus at least $\lceil\log_2 r\rceil$. A balanced binary AND tree achieves that depth. Block composition of the two conjunctions is conjunction of all $ab$ variables. Finally, for real $x,y$, $\lceil x\rceil+\lceil y\rceil-1\le\lceil x+y\rceil\le\lceil x\rceil+\lceil y\rceil$, proving the rounding assertion.
\end{proof}
The exact loss can occur: $a=3,b=5$ gives depths 2 and 3 separately, but the composed conjunction has depth 4. This does not refute a conjecture allowing lower-order terms. The attempted general lower-bound route stalls because dependence on all $ab$ variables gives only $\log_2(ab)$, not a lower bound involving the actual potentially larger complexities $D(f)$ and $D(g)$. The substitution upper bound has no corresponding reverse-substitution proof here.

\subsection{Verification and remaining gaps}
Balanced-tree depths and the rounding difference were enumerated for $1\le a,b\le64$. The proof uses the stated bounded-fan-in convention, and counts unary NOT gates although conjunctions need none. No unrestricted KRW lower bound, formalization of its unspecified lower-order term, or claim about later restricted composition theorems is obtained.

